{
  // `core` is PURE, and this config is the half of that claim a COMPILER can hold.
  //
  // The claim (`CLAUDE.md`): "PURE: no I/O, no clock, no network, and no `node:` imports -- it runs
  // in a browser renderer too". It is load-bearing rather than tidy -- the review window imports
  // `core` directly, and a `node:` import breaks the window's bundle at load, which is the one
  // failure mode where the reviewer sees nothing at all.
  //
  // `types: []` admits no ambient declarations and `lib: ES2022` admits no host globals, so
  // `setTimeout`, `crypto`, `fetch`, `process`, `Buffer` and `import.meta.dir` are each a compile
  // error here. The root config cannot do this: it covers `cli/`, `adapters/` and
  // `surface/harness/`, which legitimately use all of them, and its `bun-types` DECLARES every
  // `node:` module, so `import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'` in `core/src` typechecks clean
  // there. Both configs check `core/src`; only this one can refuse those names.
  //
  // What it CANNOT see, and why `core/test/purity.test.ts` exists as well: `Date.now()`, a
  // zero-argument `new Date()` and `Math.random` are ordinary ES2022 and legal under any config.
  // Only a text scan reaches those three. The test keeps the rest as well, deliberately doubled --
  // it costs 2 ms and it covers files this config's `include` might one day stop covering.
  //
  // It covers all of `core/src`, the converter in `core/src/adf/` included: the rule is draftgate's
  // own, and it holds for every file the window may load.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "types": [],
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  // Shipped code only. The tests import `bun:test`, and the root config covers them.
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["src/adf/__tests__"]
}
